   "summary": "A court on Monday held arrest warrant hearings for eight National Election Commission (NEC) officials accused of manipulating voter turnout numbers or tampering with vote count records during the June 3 local elections. Three officials from the NEC headquarters appeared at the Seoul Central District Court in the morning, refusing to answer reporters' questions before entering the courtroom. Hearings for the remaining five officials from regional NEC offices were set to be held later in the afternoon.",
   "summary_zh": "一家法院周一就针对8名独立国家选举委员会（NEC）官员的逮捕令举行听证，这些官员被指控在6月3日地方选举中操纵投票率数据或篡改计票记录。NEC总部的3名官员上午出现在首尔中央地方法院，进入法庭前拒绝回答记者提问。其余5名来自地方NEC办公室的官员的听证定于当天下午稍晚举行。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "A court on Monday held arrest warrant hearings for eight National Election Commission (NEC) officials accused of manipulating voter turnout numbers or tampering with vote count records during the June 3 local elections. Three officials from the NEC headquarters appeared at the Seoul Central District Court in the morning, refusing to answer reporters' questions before entering the courtroom. Hearings for the remaining five officials from regional NEC offices were set to be held later in the afternoon. The court was expected to decide whether to issue the warrants later in the day at the earliest. Requests for the warrants were filed Wednesday by special counsel Lee Tae-han's team on suspicions that the election officials entered false voter turnout figures while voting was under way or forged vote count sheets during the race for the education superintendent of Gyeonggi Province, with some of them allegedly involved in multiple cases. The move marked the first time arrest warrants have been sought since an investigation began into alleged irregularities surrounding the June elections, including unprecedented ballot shortages. According to investigators, 30 local election commissions reported inaccurate turnout numbers while polls were open, though the final turnout tallies were later corrected. The special counsel uncovered the irregularities after examining official turnout logs and questioning more than 70 election workers. In a separate case, the team suspects an election worker in the city of Gwangju in Gyeonggi Province hid and altered a vote tally sheet for the provincial education superintendent race. The local commission is also accused of attempting to cover up the incident, which led to an inaccurate final vote count",
