   "summary": "The Chief Executive Officer of the Ghana Shippers’ Authority (GSA) Prof. Ransford Gyampo has announced that the Boankra Integrated Logistics Terminal (BILT), popularly known as the Boankra Inland Port, has reached 85 per cent completion. “The Authority therefore continued the development of strategic logistics infrastructure, including the Akatekyiwa Freight Park, which is approximately 70% complete, and the Boankra Integrated Logistics Terminal – 85% complete,” he said.",
   "summary_zh": "加纳航运管理局（GSA）首席执行官兰斯福德·贾姆波教授宣布 popularly known as the Boankra Inland Port 的博安克拉综合物流码头（BILT）已完成85%。他说：“因此，该管理局继续建设战略物流基础设施，其中包括已完成约70%的阿卡特基瓦货运园区，以及完成85%的博安克拉综合物流码头。”",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "The Chief Executive Officer of the Ghana Shippers’ Authority (GSA) Prof. Ransford Gyampo has announced that the Boankra Integrated Logistics Terminal (BILT), popularly known as the Boankra Inland Port, has reached 85 per cent completion. “The Authority therefore continued the development of strategic logistics infrastructure, including the Akatekyiwa Freight Park, which is approximately 70% complete, and the Boankra Integrated Logistics Terminal – 85% complete,” he said. He said the projected when completed will be Ghana’s flagship dry port — an inland terminal designed to move cargo away from the congested coastal gateways and closer to shippers in the middle and north of the country. Covering about 413 acres near Kumasi in the Ashanti Region, the Boankra Inland Port will serve as a dry port, allowing importers and exporters outside the coastal capital to clear goods closer to their final destinations, thereby driving regional economic development and job creation. The multi-million dollar terminal is designed to serve as a major inland logistics hub linking the Tema and Takoradi seaports to Ghana’s middle and northern regions, as well as to landlocked neighbours including Burkina Faso, Mali, and Niger. Prof. Gyampo said delays from excessive border formalities, multiple checkpoints and poor coordination increase vehicle operating costs, demurrage charges and cargo deterioration — and weaken Ghana’s competitiveness as a regional transit hub",
