   "summary": "Unlock these with Subscription The wind made its presence felt for the first time in the Test, and India made decent use of the conditions to pick up two wickets with the soft ball and keep its hopes of a rare victory in New Zealand alive.",
   "summary_zh": "订阅即可解锁这些内容。本场测试赛首次感受到了风的力量，印度队充分利用这一条件，借助软球连取2个 wickets，使自己罕见地在新西兰获胜的希望得以延续。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Account subscription benefits alongside Premium Stories, Editorials, Opinions and more. Unlock these with Subscription The wind made its presence felt for the first time in the Test, and India made decent use of the conditions to pick up two wickets with the soft ball and keep its hopes of a rare victory in New Zealand alive. Washington Sundar and Prasidh Krishna struck to remove the two overnight unbeaten batters as New Zealand went into lunch at 157 for four, still 195 runs short of wiping out India’s first-innings deficit, on the final day of the first Test at Basin Reserve on Monday (November 23, 2026). With the new ball still 35 overs away and eight wickets to get, Jasprit Bumrah was used in short bursts. It was Washington, however, who found the breakthrough in the 12 th over of the day, making the most of the wind blowing into him. The off-spinner got one to drift into Devon Conway, the inside edge lobbed off the pad, allowing Rishabh Pant to stretch forward to his left and pouch a neat catch. Conway, who had battled through the final session on Sunday (November 22), scored 65 (165b, 7x4). Four overs later, Prasidh got the better of Rachin Ravindra (37, 129b, 4x4). The left-hander, who had looked comfortable through the morning, pushed at a delivery in the channel outside off, offering K.L. Rahul the opportunity to stretch to his left in the air at widish second slip, the only fielder in the cordon, and take the catch. Daryl Mitchell and Tom Blundell, however, were in no mood to let the session slip away. The pair negotiated the remaining overs without much fuss, keeping India’s attack at bay and ensuring there was no further damage before lunch. With the new ball nine overs away, India will hope the harder cherry brings the extra pace, bounce and movement needed to prise the remaining wickets. Comments have to be in English, and in full sentences. They cannot be abusive or personal. Please abide by our community guidelines for posting your comments. We have migrated to a new commenting platform. If you are already a registered user of The Hindu and logged in, you may continue to engage with our articles. If you do not have an account please register and login to post comments. Users can access their older comments by logging into their accounts on Vuukle",
