   "title": "ECO: Nigeria Seeks Greater Say in ECOWAS Currency Governance",
   "url": "https://leadership.ng/eco-nigeria-seeks-greater-say-in-ecowas-currency-governance/",
   "domain": "leadership.ng",
   "source": "Leadership（尼日利亚）",
   "published": "Sun, 22 Nov 2026 17:50:21 +0000",
   "_dt": 1795369821.0,
   "ageDays": 0.71,
   "summary": "The Governor of the Central Bank of Nigeria, Olayemi Cardoso made the call at the Ministerial Session of the 6th Presidential Task Force Meeting on the ECOWAS Single Currency Programme in Abidjan, Cote d’ Ivoire. He restated Nigeria’s commitment to the ECOWAS Monetary Cooperation Programme and to establishing the ECO, describing it as “longstanding and unwavering” and citing substantial financial contributions, sustained technical support, and compromises made “in the broader interest of regional consensus.",
   "summary_zh": "尼日利亚中央银行行长Olayemi Cardoso在科特迪瓦阿比让举行的西非国家经济共同体（ECOWAS）单一货币计划第六次总统工作组会议部长级会议上提出这一主张。他重申尼日利亚对ECOWAS货币合作计划以及建立经济共同体（ECO）的支持，称其是“长期而坚定不移的”，并列举了尼日利亚提供的巨额资金贡献、持续的技术支持，以及为“达成更广泛的区域共识而作出的妥协”。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "The Governor of the Central Bank of Nigeria, Olayemi Cardoso made the call at the Ministerial Session of the 6th Presidential Task Force Meeting on the ECOWAS Single Currency Programme in Abidjan, Cote d’ Ivoire. The Ministerial Committee of the ECOWAS Presidential Task Force comprises Ministers of Finance and Governors of central banks of PTF member states. He restated Nigeria’s commitment to the ECOWAS Monetary Cooperation Programme and to establishing the ECO, describing it as “longstanding and unwavering” and citing substantial financial contributions, sustained technical support, and compromises made “in the broader interest of regional consensus. “Nigeria has and will continue to demonstrate solidarity and fairness, respecting the sovereignty of other member states. We remain fully committed to the successful establishment of the ECO and to a Monetary Union that is credible, sustainable and beneficial to all Member States,” Cardoso stated. He, however, maintained that solidarity must be accompanied by equity, arguing that countries with larger economies and greater financial responsibilities should be given fair consideration in the governance of the institutions they support. “While bigger economies continue to take up the burden, they should also be considered to play a significant role because of the risk and systemic importance they have in the sub-region. Above all, such economies need to defend the huge contribution they make before their respective parliaments and the civil society. Where obligations are shared but influence is not, arrangements may be agreed, but their long-term sustainability may be difficult to guarantee,” he added. Reiterating Nigeria’s position, Cardoso emphasised that the country’s stance was not driven by dominance or entitlement but by a sense of responsibility stemming from its status as the region’s largest economy and one of the principal contributors to the proposed Union. He noted that Nigeria “will commit substantial financial resources, reserves and institutional capital to the success of the Monetary Union”, adding that it is therefore reasonable that responsibility, accountability and participation in governance should be appropriately aligned with the scale of those commitments. Cardoso thus urged delegates to approach the deliberations with openness, mutual respect and a willingness to compromise, and expressed confidence that a balanced outcome could be achieved through a shared commitment to building a strong, stable and prosperous Monetary Union. We’ve got the edge. Get real-time reports, breaking scoops, and exclusive angles delivered straight to your phone. Don’t settle for stale news. Join LEADERSHIP NEWS on WhatsApp for 24/7 updates →",
